### Date format

The **Date format** field is a list of calendars. Select one to see the dates of the texts converted to that calendar. The available calendars are:
- **Gregorian**: the civil calendar mostly used in the Western part of the world. It was introduced in <htdate0> by Pope Gregory XIII to replace the Julian calendar and correct the drift of the equinoxes. The reform adjusted the leap years and removed <htdate1>, and it was adopted gradually in different countries;
- **Hebrew**: the lunisolar calendar of the Jewish tradition;
- **Hispanic**: the era used in the Iberian Peninsula, counted from 38 B.C.;
- **Islamic**: the lunar calendar of the Islamic tradition;
- **Julian (Days)**: the Julian Day number, that is, the days counted from 1 January 4713 B.C.;
- **Mesoamerican**: the Mayan Long Count, with the Haab and Tzolkin cycles;
- **Mesoamerican (Ext.)**: an extended version of the Mayan Long Count;
- **Persian**: the Solar Hijri calendar used in Iran and Afghanistan;
- **French Rev.**: the calendar of the French Revolution;
- **Shaka**: the Saka calendar, the national calendar of India;
- **Chinese**: the Chinese lunisolar calendar, showing the stem, the branch, and the animal of the year;
- **Aymara**: the calendar of the Aymara people of the Andes;
- **Mapuche**: the calendar of the Mapuche people of South America;
- **Inca**: the calendar of the Inca Empire;
- **Javanese**: the calendar of Java, in Indonesia;
- **Japanese**: the Japanese calendar, with the name of the era (nengo).

The same field is useful when comparing documents written in different calendars: choose the same format for all of them.
